DESIGN RATIONALE
- Only Patient, Practitioner, and Appointment were retained as core domain classes because they represent real-world entities with persistent states and behavior. Technical elements like Database and primitive attributes like Name or Status were excluded from being classes. Operations were assigned directly to the entities that own the data: Appointment handles its own status updates and cancellations, while Patient and Practitioner manage their respective booking requests and availability.
- Regarding relationships, Patient has a simple association with Appointment with a multiplicity of 1 to 0..*, meaning a patient can book multiple appointments, but does not own the appointment lifecycle. Practitioner has a simple association with Appointment with a multiplicity of 1 to 0..*, meaning a practitioner conducts multiple appointments, but each slot has one primary practitioner. Inheritance was rejected because an Appointment is an event while a Patient is a person. Forcing Clinic to own all objects was also rejected to prevent creating a centralized God Object.

